m Seven Pairs.\n\nSo preserving closed hand is valuable.\n\nAdditionally, Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) requires no exposure and self-draw win.\n\nBut I\u2019ve already discarded F3 and B5 \u2014 but those are normal plays.\n\nFully Concealed Hand is about melds, not discards.\n\n\"Fully Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be concealed, and the winning tile is drawn by the winner.\"\n\nSo if I never claim a tile to form a meld, and win by self-draw, I get 4 points.\n\nBut if I Chi now, I break that.\n\nSimilarly, Concealed Hand (2 points) is for concealed melds but win by claiming \u2014 but again, if I Chi, I have an exposed meld.\n\nSo for maximizing score, staying concealed is better.\n\nNow, what about the current opportunity?\n\nIs there a compelling reason to Chi?\n\nSuppose I don\u2019t Chi, I pass.\n\nThen I keep flexibility.\n\nI can later draw and try to improve pairs.\n\nFor example, if I draw B1, B7, B8, W2, W3, W6, T1, T3, T4, T9 \u2014 any duplicate helps.\n\nEven T1, T3, etc.\n\nUnshown tiles show T1:4 (all unseen? Probably some seen), but we don\u2019t know visibility \u2014 assume all counts are remaining.\n\nT1:4 left \u2014 plenty.\n\nBut risk: another player might win.\n\nBut no strong signal.\n\nAlternatively, if I Chi, I commit to a melded hand.\n\nWhich direction?\n\nChi T3 to form [T2,T3,T4] uses T3 and T4 \u2014 keeps T1, which could pair later or be used in W-suit? No.\n\nT1 is isolated.\n\nBut B-suit has many pairs \u2014 perhaps focus on that.\n\nBut I can\u2019t influence draws much.\n\nAnother consideration: the next player has [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 middle bamboo \u2014 so B2, B6 might be dangerous to discard later.\n\nBut not relevant now.\n\nBack to decision: preserve Seven Pairs potential or commit to melded hand.\n\nSeven Pairs: 24 points \u2014 good base.\n\nCan I get additional patterns?\n\nIf I have Seven Pairs, I can also have:\n- All Even Pungs? No, pairs not pungs.\n- Tile Hog