elded hand is suboptimal.\n\nThus, I should pass to preserve the possibility.\n\nBut is Seven Pairs really achievable?\n\nI have three pairs now \u2014 need four more.\n\nThere are 14 tiles needed for seven pairs \u2014 I have 13, so after drawing one, I\u2019ll have 14.\n\nBut I need the right ones.\n\nExpected value: many tiles left.\n\nBut even if not completed, keeping options open is better than committing to inferior path.\n\nMoreover, I can always discard a pair later if I get too many singles, but once I expose a meld, I can\u2019t undo.\n\nTherefore, passing is better.\n\nBut let\u2019s confirm valid actions: [Chi T2; Chi T3; Pass]\n\nSo Pass is allowed.\n\nFinal decision: Pass.\n\nAnswer should be \"Pass\".\n\nBut let\u2019s make sure.\n\nPerhaps Chi T3 to form [T2,T3,T4] is good because T4 is less useful \u2014 but still, commitment cost is high.\n\nNo, the strategic cost outweighs the minor benefit.\n\nEvaluation: The hand has strong potential for Seven Pairs (24 points) with existing pairs in B1, B7, and B8. Forming a chow now would forfeit this high-value pattern and commit to a lower-scoring melded hand. Although claiming T2 to form a chow is possible, preserving hand flexibility maximizes long-term winning potential.\n\nReason: Avoiding meld exposure keeps Seven Pairs and Fully Concealed Hand possibilities alive, which offer significantly higher scores than any immediate chow-based patterns.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
