tterns. My hand includes:\n- A complete Pung of B7 (B7\u00d73)\n- A pair of B8\n- A nearly complete Chow in Bamboos: B7-B8, needing B6 or B9 to extend\n- A possible terminal-based hand with T1, T2, T3 (potential Chow), T9 (terminal), and B1\u00d72 (pair candidate)\n\nNotable scoring opportunities:\n- **Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point)**: B7 is a terminal pung (7 is not terminal; only 1 or 9). So this does **not** count.\n- **Outside Hand (4 points)** requires all melds and the pair to contain terminals or honors. Currently, my [W1,W2,W3] is mixed (contains middle tiles), so it breaks Outside Hand unless replaced.\n- If I discard a middle tile like T2 or T1, I risk breaking potential, but holding T1,T2,T3 gives me a **Pure Double Chow** (1 point) if I form two identical chows.\n- B7 pung is strong \u2014 could aim for **Upper Tiles (24 points)** since B7, B8 are high-numbered, but B1 and T1-T3 are low, conflicting.\n- Alternatively, focus on **All Simples (2 points)** \u2014 but B1, B7, T1, T9, B1 are terminals \u2014 too many terminals.\n\nWait types:\n- Discarding B1 would remove a terminal pair possibility.\n- Keeping B7\u00d73 and B8\u00d72 suggests aiming for **Upper Tiles**, but current hand has B1\u00d72 and T1,T2,T3,T9 \u2014 too many low/terminal tiles.\n\nBest chance: **Seven Pairs (24 points)**?\nCurrent pairs or triples:\n- B7\u00d73 \u2192 one pair + one single\n- B8\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\n- B1\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\nThat\u2019s **three pairs** already. If I can draw or claim to make more pairs, it's promising.\n\nBut I also have T1,T2,T3,T9 \u2014 four singles. Not ideal.\n\nAlternatively, consider **Half Flush (6 points)**: honor + one suit. Could go for **Bamboo-heavy** hand? I have B1\u00d72, B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72 \u2014 that\u2019s 7 Bamboo tiles. But W1-W2-W3 is Characters \u2014 different suit, already melded. Can't change.\n\nSo must work around [W1,W2,W3] as fixed.\n\nReevaluate: \nHold T1,T2,T3 \u2192 can form Chow [T1,T2,T3] (1 point via **All Chows**, possibly **Mixed Straight** if other chows align).\nAlso T9 is a terminal \u2014 could be useful for **Two Terminal Chows** if I had [T7,T8,T9], but I don\u2019t.\n\nBetter: **Chicken Hand (8 points)** may be reachable if no other pattern scores, but we need at least 8 points.\n\nLet\u2019s assess:\n- Already have a meld: [W1,W2,W3] (Characters chow)\n- Hold: T1,T2,T3 \u2192 can form [T1,T2,T3] chow (Bamboos) \u2014 same numbers, different suit \u2192 **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)** if I get another chow of 1,2,3 or 7,8,9? No \u2014 need three chows of same number across suits.\n\nWait: **Mixed Triple Chow** requires three chows of same sequence (e.g., 5,6,7) in different suits. Here, [W1,W2,W3] and [T1,T2,T3] would be two such chows. If I can form a third in Dots, that\u2019s **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)**.\n\nCurrently, I have B1\u00d72 \u2014 could form [B1,B2,B3] if I draw B2/B3, but B2 is rare (only 2 left), B3: 3 left.\n\nBut B1\u00d72 is only a pair now \u2014 no B2/B3 in hand.\n\nSo **Mixed Triple Chow** is possible but unlikely due to lack of B2/B3.\n\nAlternative: **Three-Suited Terminal Chows (16 points)** \u2014 needs:\n- Two chows of 1,2,3 in one suit\n- Two chows of 7,8,9 in second suit\n- Pair of 5 in third suit\n\nNot matching.\n\nBack to Seven Pairs idea:\nCurrent pairs:\n- B7\u00d73 \u2192 counts as one pair (extra tile single)\n- B8\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\n- B1\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\n\u2192 3 pairs\n\nThen T1, T2, T3, T9 \u2014 all singles.\n\nIf I discard one of the B7, I break the pung \u2014 but then I\u2019d have B7\u00d72 \u2192 pair, still three pairs.\n\nBut I cannot create seven pairs from here easily.\n\nBetter: stick with **All Chows (2 points)** + **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)** if I form [T1,T2,T3].\n\nI already have [W1,W2,W3], and if I form [T1,T2,T3], that\u2019s two chows of 1-2-3 in different suits.\n\nNeed a third chow of 1-2-3 in Dots \u2014 possible if I draw B2/B3, but again, limited.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **Mixed Triple Chow** only requires **three chows** of same numbers in **different suits** \u2014 e.g., one in each suit.\n\nSo [W1,W2,W3], [T1,T2,T3], and [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 perfect.\n\nI have B1\u00d72 \u2014 so if I draw B2 or B3, I can complete it.\n\nBut currently, I need to keep B1.\n\nHowever, I have B7\u00d73 \u2014 very strong. Should I break potential?\n\nAlternatively, consider **Upper Tiles (24 points)**: all tiles 7,8,9.\n\nI have:\n- B7\u00d73 \u2192 good\n- B8\u00d72 \u2192 good\n- T9 \u2192 good\n- But T1,T2,T3, B1 \u2014 all low, bad\n\nSo unless I discard all low tiles, not feasible.\n\nThus, best path: aim for **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)** by forming [T1,T2,T3] and hoping to draw B2/B3 for [B1,B2,B3].\n\nBut I only have B1\u00d72 \u2014 need B2 and B3.\n\nUnshown B2: 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nBut I also have B7\u00d73 \u2014 which is worth keeping?\n\nWait: if I keep B7\u00d73, I can use it as a pung \u2014 but for what?\n\n**Pung of Terminals or Honors**: B7 is not terminal (only 1 or 9) \u2014 so no.\n\n**All Even Pungs**? 7 is odd.\n\nNo pattern values B7 pung highly.\n\nBut **Upper Tiles (24 points)** requires all tiles \u22657 \u2014 B7, B8, B9, T7, T8, T9, W7, W8, W9.\n\nI have:\n- B7\u00d73\n- B8\u00d72\n- T9\n- Missing: B9 (2 left), T7/T8 (T7:4, T8:4), W7/W8/W9\n\nBut I have no T7/T8, and W1-W2-W3 is melded \u2014 all low.\n\nSo **Upper Tiles** impossible.\n\nThus, B7 pung is not contributing to high-scoring hand.\n\nSimilarly, B8 pair is just a pair.\n\nSo better to break away from B7 and aim for **Mixed Triple Chow**?\n\nBut I need B2/B3 \u2014 not in hand.\n\nAlternative: **Seven Pairs**?\n\nLet\u2019s list all tiles in hand:\n- T1, T2, T3, T9 \u2014 four distinct singles\n- B1\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\n- B7\u00d73 \u2192 one pair + one extra\n- B8\u00d72 \u2192 one pair\n\nTotal unique: T1, T2, T3, T9, B1 (paired), B7 (tripled), B8 (paired)\n\nIf I discard T9, T3, T2, or T1, I reduce clutter.\n\nBut T1,T2,T3 together can form a chow.\n\nBest immediate value: **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)** with [W1,W2,W3], [T1,T2,T3], and future [B1,B2,B3].\n\nI have B1\u00d72 \u2014 so if I draw B2 or B3, I can Chi.\n\nBut I need to keep B1.\n\nAlso, T1,T2,T3 \u2014 keep all.\n\nT9 is useless here \u2014 doesn't help 1-2-3 chow.\n\nSo **discard T9**.\n\nOther option: discard B7 \u2014 but it's part of a pung, might be useful later, but no high pattern.\n\nBut if I keep B7\u00d73, and someone discards B6 or B8, I could form a chow? But B7-B8-B9 or B6-B7-B8 \u2014 I have B8\u00d72, so [B6,B7,B8] or [B7,B8,B9] possible.\n\nBut B9 only 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nAnd I have B7\u00d73 \u2014 if I promote to Kong, but not helpful.\n\nStill, **Upper Four (12 points)**: tiles 6,7,8,9.\n\nI have B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, T9 \u2014 could be building, but T1,T2,T3,B1 conflict.\n\nUnless I discard all low tiles.\n\nBut then I lose [W1,W2,W3] \u2014 already melded, stuck.\n\nSo cannot pursue Upper Four.\n\nThus, most consistent path is **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)** + possibly **All Chows (2 points)** + maybe **No Honors (1 point)** since no honors in hand.\n\nTotal: 8+2+1=11 \u2014 sufficient.\n\nTo achieve, need to form [T1,T2,T3] and keep B1 for future [B1,B2,B3].\n\nSo must keep T1,T2,T3,B1.\n\nB7\u00d73 \u2014 can discard one B7? But it\u2019s a pung \u2014 if I discard, I break it.\n\nBut I can only play one tile.\n\nAvailable plays: Play T2, T1, T3, T9, B1, B7, B8.\n\nIf I play B7, I break the pung \u2014 but still have two left \u2014 can form pair later? No, need three for pung.\n\nAfter discard, I have B7\u00d72 \u2014 which is just a pair.\n\nBut in Seven Pairs, that\u2019s fine.\n\nBut earlier analysis shows Seven Pairs unlikely.\n\nBetter to keep B7\u00d73 for now.\n\nBut T9 is completely useless for Mixed Triple Chow \u2014 only use would be in [T7,T8,T9], but I have no T7/T8.\n\nSo T9 is dead tile.\n\nTherefore, **Play T9** is safest discard.\n\nIt doesn't contribute to any chow in hand, not part of a pair, not helping any major pattern.\n\nWhereas T1,T2,T3 together form a chow.\n\nB1\u00d72 is a pair and start of [B1,B2,B3].\n\nB7\u00d73 is a strong pung, though not high-scoring.\n\nB8\u00d72 is a pair.\n\nSo only T9 is truly disposable.\n\nMoreover, playing T9 avoids giving away useful tiles \u2014 T1,T2,T3 might be needed by others.\n\nAlso, unshown T9: 3 left \u2014 so not rare.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding T9 removes a useless terminal tile that doesn't contribute to any viable high-scoring pattern. Keeping T1, T2, T3 allows for forming a Chow to support **Mixed Triple Chow (8 points)** when combined with the existing [W1,W2,W3] and potential [B1,B2,B3]. Holding B1, B7, and B8 maintains flexibility for pairs or chows. No higher-value pattern is currently feasible without changing core structure.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
